1
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
In music she found her dream, her love, herself.

2
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Glitter is a drama, music, romance film directed by Vondie Curtis-Hall.

3
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:30,000
A young woman is catapulted into pop stardom, with her
already-famous DJ boyfriend calling the shots.

4
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Billie Frank, played by Mariah Carey.

5
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Julian 'Dice' Black, played by Max Beesley.

6
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:54,000
Louise, played by Da Brat.

7
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:22,000
Roxanne, played by Tia Texada.

8
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:50,000
Lillian Frank, played by Valarie Pettiford.

9
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:18,000
Kelly, played by Ann Magnuson.

10
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:46,000
Glitter is a 2001 American romantic musical drama film written by
Kate Lanier and directed by Vondie Curtis-Hall.

11
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:14,000
It stars Mariah Carey, Max Beesley, and Terrence Howard.

12
00:41:36,000 --> 00:41:42,000
Carey plays Billie Frank, an aspiring singer who, along with her friends Louise
(rapper Da Brat) and Roxanne (Tia Texada), is a club dancer.

13
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:10,000
Music producer Timothy Walker (Howard) offers them a contract
as backup singers/dancers to another singer.

14
00:48:32,000 --> 00:48:38,000
At the premiere of the song they record, Billie meets Julian "Dice" Black
(Beesley), a nightclub DJ who helps establish her solo career.

15
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:06,000
In the process, Billie and Dice fall in love.

16
00:55:28,000 --> 00:55:34,000
Carey began working on a film and soundtrack project titled All That Glitters in 1997, but
it was put on hold in favor of other commitments with her record label.

17
00:58:56,000 --> 00:59:02,000
Following this, she aimed to complete the film and
album project for the summer of 2001.

18
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:30,000
Shooting began in Toronto and New York City at the end of September 2000.

19
01:05:52,000 --> 01:05:58,000
Carey used the time to work on the soundtrack of the film, along with
Eric Benét and Da Brat, who also appeared in the film.

20
01:09:20,000 --> 01:09:26,000
The film and its accompanying soundtrack's release dates were delayed after Carey was suddenly
hospitalized, citing "extreme exhaustion" and a "physical and emotional breakdown".

21
01:12:48,000 --> 01:12:54,000
The film was released on September 21, 2001 by 20th Century Fox in the United States and Canada and by Columbia TriStar Film
Distributors International in international markets, ten days after the release of the soundtrack on September 11, 2001.

22
01:16:16,000 --> 01:16:22,000
Glitter was widely panned by critics and audiences and was a box-office bomb.

23
01:19:44,000 --> 01:19:50,000
Reviewers were highly disappointed with the film, along with Carey's
performance, earning her a Golden Raspberry Award for worst actress.

24
01:23:12,000 --> 01:23:18,000
This also caused the film to receive negative commentary on social media sites, with
Carey herself later admitting that she regretted being part of the film.

25
01:26:40,000 --> 01:26:46,000
Some went on to call it one of the worst films ever made.

26
01:30:08,000 --> 01:30:14,000
Glitter opened in 1,996 American theaters, and grossed $2.5 million in its first week,
with a worldwide total of $5.3 million against a $22 million budget.

27
01:33:36,000 --> 01:33:42,000
The soundtrack of the film achieved moderate commercial success and went on to sell over two
million copies worldwide, considerably less than Carey's previous releases.

28
01:37:04,000 --> 01:37:10,000
The film explores themes of talent, career woman, talent show, movie star.

29
01:40:32,000 --> 01:40:38,000
The tone is emotional.
